On Nov 15, 12:07 pm, Janis Papanagnou <janis_papanag...@hotmail.com>
wrote:
> James wrote:
> > On Nov 15, 2:54 am, Janis Papanagnou
> > <janis_papanag...@hotmail.com> wrote:

> >>And mumaks can appear quite early; I typically meet them at
> >>the oracle level or in mine town.

> > Yes and no.  Not leveling up too quickly can reduce the
> > probability of this enormously.

> You mean deliberately restricting yourself from evolving with
> your XL, thus restricting your character from HPs/maxHPs
> gains, and somtimes restricting yourself also from getting
> race specific level-dependent intrinsics, just because a mumak
> might appear?  Quasi a "mini-protection racket"; well, to each
> his own. But mind that with less HPs your two-hit limit that
> you mention below is significantly more difficult to achieve.

Not really.  What I mean is trying to keep yourself more or less
balanced.  In the early game, I'll usually give priority to my
pet, when it comes to killing monsters and uping XL, and for
myself, I will try to get decent armor before uping my XL too
much.  Hit points can come from other sources than just uping
XP.  And mumaks, per se, don't really have much to do with it;
it's just that following my usual strategy, I don't usually
start seeing them before I can handle them.  (But the strategy
does adopt to the situation---if I'm a legal with a long sword,
for example, I'll generally try to reach XP 6 fairly quickly,
for Excalibur.)

Anyway, what I see in my games is that I'll almost always reach
minetown before mumaks can appear, and usually Sobokan as well
(unless the Oracle level is particularly deep, level 8 or 9).
Simply by following my usual strategy.  Which usually includes
avoiding the opposite extreme as well---I don't normally go down
more than one or two levels more than my XP, unless I've been
lucky finding very good armor very early.

> > It's a delicate balance, but I

> IMO the game is balanced at XL:4 as well (or as bad if some
> like) as it is at XL:8.

IMHO, if you're at XL:8 with an AC of 8, it's decidely
unbalanced.  By the time I get to XL:8, I generally want my AC
to be negative, and usually will have already gotten some extra
hitpoints by quafing.  (And at XL:4, I'm not going to be seeing
mumaks before level 12.)

> > don't like to level up too high until I've gotten enough AC
> > to be able to take, say, at least two hits by a mumak.

> Well, YMMV, but in my experience the mumak butt is so
> effective that even with a better AC you don't want to get too
> many (i.e. more than two, or even one) hits from it; and I am
> rarely at the AC:-10 point at that stage of the game.

I don't know the exact formulas, but my impression is that
somewhere between AC:0 and AC:-5, there is a line: getting to
that point is very important, going beyond it, generally less
so, until much later.  Even in that range, I'd rather AC:-2 with
speed boots than AC:-3 with iron shoes.  And of course, more hit
points help.

The point is, however, that if I'm only XP4, I won't be seeing
any mumaks.

> While I don't consider levelling up as a priority for per se I
> also don't restrict myself to not evolve. The point is that,
> as soon as mumaks appear (or soldier ants) you're at the
> (latest) point to switch combat tactics; no melee but ranged.
> (Things can be different, though, if you got lucky with you
> equipment with AC and melee artifacts, but ranged combat is
> generally good in case of monsters doing huge damage.)

Certainly.  Even more than AC, speed and knowledge are winners;
if you're fast enough, and know that the mumaks are there soon
enough, there are generally ways to handle the situation.  The
AC is really just a limited assurance for the unexpected, when
it comes to mumaks.  (This is less true for faster monsters that
come in packs.  Or monsters that can wield weapons or use attack
wands.  Mumaks worry me less than woodland elves with wands.
Which I often do see in minetown.)

> > And I've gotten some means of reacting after the first:
> > sufficient attack power to kill it, speed to get away,
> > instant Elbereth, etc.

> With mumaks you want to avoid melee range anyway, so I'd want
> to do as much ranged attacks as possible before he's adjacent,
> and the E-word is mandatory (but often unreliable).

I don't know.  While mumaks hit hard, they go down pretty easy.
If I've got any sort of speed at all (not being burdened is
usually enough), I'll use melee, hitting it, then stepping back
and waiting for it to come to me.  I'm probably playing with
fire doing that, but it's worked pretty well for me until now.

> > mumaks are only difficulty level 7, and Oracle can be as low
> > as level 9 (which means that you have to be less that XP5 to
> > be sure that they won't show up).  But that's what I *like*
> > to do.

> Yeah, to each his own. But even if you calculate your XL for a
> certain dungeon level, say 9 in your example, you have to
> proceed anyway. And you usually won't be safer from mumaks on
> dungeon level 10 or 11 if you don't switch tactics; the damage
> they do is too large.

I don't know why, but I find mumaks overrated.  A long time ago,
I used to fall to them a lot, but that was back when I'd just
level up as fast as possible and bang away as hard as possible.
Since I've started thinking, managing my pet (which means that I
don't level up as quickly, since it's getting most of the easy
kills), trying on every armor my pet will step on, etc., they
don't seem to be a major cause of death.  Unlike elves with
wands.

